      ":question/problem": "Using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{17\\pi}{12}=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}+\\frac{2\\pi}{3},\n$$\n\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\cos\\left(\\frac{17\\pi}{12}\\right)$.\n\n$\\cos\\left(\\frac{17\\pi}{12}\\right)=$ {{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+y)=\\cos x\\cos y-\\sin x\\sin y.\n$$\n\nApply it to the decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{17\\pi}{12}\\right)=\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}+\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)-\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)\\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstitute the signed special-angle values and combine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\\n&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
